        "alt": "For many years, the fashion industry has been driven by speed, scale, and low prices. But the conversation is slowly changing.\n\nToday, consumers are becoming more conscious about where their clothes come from, how they are made, and how long they last.\nInterestingly, many of the ideas now promoted as “sustainable fashion” have existed in India for centuries.\n\nOur textile traditions have always been built around principles like:\n• Handcrafted production\n• Natural materials and dyes\n• Local artisan communities\n\nTechniques such as Ajrakh block printing, handloom weaving, and traditional embroidery are naturally slower processes, but they create products that carry depth, craftsmanship, and cultural value.\nWhat the world now calls slow fashion has long been part of India’s textile heritage.\nFor textile entrepreneurs, this shift presents a powerful opportunity. As global consumers move toward authenticity, transparency, and responsible fashion, handcrafted textiles from India can find a stronger place in both domestic and international markets.\n\nHowever, the real future lies in combining traditional craft with modern business thinking, strong production systems, thoughtful design, and meaningful storytelling.\n\nIndia already has the craftsmanship. What we need is entrepreneurial vision to bring these traditions into the modern fashion ecosystem.\n\nPersonally, this is something I strongly believe in and try to contribute to through my work at @sarjantextiles Building products around authentic Indian textiles and craftsmanship while adapting them for today’s market.\n\nCurious to hear from others in the textile and fashion industry:\nDo you think India can lead the global sustainable fashion movement?\n\n#ajrakh #ajrakhprints #ajrakhwholesale\n[Ajrakh Block Print, Handicrafts, Sustainable fashion future in India, Handmade, Natural Dye, Sarjan Textiles]",
